{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1554 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b160b1c770afa25e5a7ff89f4fa08ec33cc0c5c7853e33776216aa40930032bc" :edb/db-before-t 1553 :edb/db-after-t 1554 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534866 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:16:03.069Z" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191093 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191094 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191095 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191096 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191097 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441700 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441711 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441717 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441730 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441737 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186191092 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441711 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192389 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.4,\\, -0.3,\\, -2.2,\\, -0.4$." 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192389 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.4,\\, -0.3,\\, -2.2,\\, -0.4.$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192389 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 1\n$$\n\nfor which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}e^{- (x_{i} - θ)^{2}/2}) \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- ∑(x_{i} - θ)^{2}/2} \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- (∑x_{i}^{2} - 2θ∑x_{i} + nθ^{2})/2} \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- (C - 2Sθ + nθ^{2})/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 0.4 + (-0.3) + (-2.2) + (-0.4) \\\\\n&=-2.5 \\\\\nC &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= (0.4)^{2} + (-0.3)^{2} + (-2.2)^{2} + (-0.4)^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= (2π)^{-4/2}e^{- (5.25 - 2(-2.5)θ + 4θ^{2})/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4π^{2}}e^{-2.625 - 2.5θ - 2θ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192389 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i$ and $\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.4+(-0.3)+(-2.2)+(-0.4) \\\\  & =-2.5 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{2}+(-0.3)^{2}+(-2.2)^{2}+(-0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(5.25-2(-2.5)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{-2.625\\,-\\,2.5\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192521 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5, \\, 2.0, \\, 1.1, \\, 0.4, \\, 3.3, \\, 2.7$." 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192521 1071 "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5, \\, 2.0, \\, 1.1, \\, 0.4, \\, 3.3, \\, 2.7.$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192521 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)θe^{- θx_{i}} \\\\\n&= (θe^{- θx_{1}}) \\cdot (θe^{- θx_{2}})⋯(θe^{- θx_{n}}) \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{- θ(x_{1} + x_{2} + ⋯ + x_{n})} \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{- θ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{-Sθ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 1.5 + 2.0 + 1.1 + 0.4 + 3.3 + 2.7 \\\\\n&= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= θ^{6}e^{-11θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192521 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{1}})\\cdot (\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{2}})⋯(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+⋯+{x}_{n})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.5+2.0+1.1+0.4+3.3+2.7 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192523 1071 "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.0, \\, 4.5, \\,5.1 .$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192523 1071 "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1$,\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.0, \\, 4.5, \\,5.1$." 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192523 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)((θ + 1)x_{i}^{- θ - 2}) \\\\\n&= (θ + 1)^{n} \\cdot ∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{- θ - 2} \\\\\n&= (θ + 1)^{n}(∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{- θ - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 2.0 \\cdot 4.5 \\cdot 5.1 = 45.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = (\\theta+1)^{3} (45.9)^{-\\theta-2}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186192523 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.0\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 5.1=45.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = (\\theta+1)^{3} ( 45.9 )^{-\\theta-2}.\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441776 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441778 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441779 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441780 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441781 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441777 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441783 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441784 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441785 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441787 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441775 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441782 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441786 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441788 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186192932 1050 660.0 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186193201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186193201 1071 "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; θ) = θx^{- θ - 1}, x \\ge 1$,\nwhere $θ$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1, 3.2, 5.0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186193201 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1,\\,3.2,\\,5.0.$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186193201 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.1\\cdot 3.2\\cdot 5.0=33.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{3}(33.6)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186193897 1064 "Likelihood functions of samples drawn from populations modeled using continuous random variables are defined similarly to discrete variables. The only difference is that we use probability density functions instead of probability mass functions.\n\nIf $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed *continuous* random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nFor example, let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be continuous, I.I.D. random variables with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nHere, $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Suppose we conduct our random sample and get the following results:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2.5, \\qquad x_2 = 3.2, \\qquad x_3 = 1.2\n$$\n\nIn this case, we compute the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(θx_{i}^{- θ - 1}) \\\\\n&= (θx_{1}^{- θ - 1}) \\cdot (θx_{2}^{- θ - 1})⋯(θx_{n}^{- θ - 1}) \\\\\n&= θ^{n}(x_{1}^{- θ - 1}) \\cdot (x_{2}^{- θ - 1})⋯(x_{n}^{- θ - 1}) \\\\\n&= θ^{n}(x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}⋯x_{n})^{- θ - 1} \\\\\n&= θ^{n}(∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{- θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have $n=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 2.5 \\cdot 3.2 \\cdot 1.2 = 9.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{3} (9.6)^{-\\theta-1}\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186193897 1064 "Likelihood functions of samples drawn from populations modeled using continuous random variables are defined similarly to discrete variables. The only difference is that we use probability density functions instead of probability mass functions.\n\nIf $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed *continuous* random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be continuous, I.I.D. random variables with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nHere, $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Suppose we conduct our random sample and get the following results:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2.5, \\qquad x_2 = 3.2, \\qquad x_3 = 1.2.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, we compute the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =(\\theta {x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot (\\theta {x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯(\\theta {x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({x}_{1}^{-\\theta -1})\\cdot ({x}_{2}^{-\\theta -1})⋯({x}_{n}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}{({x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}⋯{x}_{n})}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have $n=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.5\\cdot 3.2\\cdot 1.2=9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{3} ( 9.6 )^{-\\theta-1}.\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186193902 1064 "The probability density function of a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2}\n$$\n\nSome example normal distribution curves are shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9359fc84401aabb19e526755b70647cb0496fa8ddba65974466e0ffc3dbbbdc.png)\n\nIf we set $\\sigma=1$, then $f(x)$ simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2}\n$$\n\nThis is a standard normal random variable whose mean is shifted right by $\\mu$ units.\n\nFinally, setting the unknown parameter as $\\theta$ when discussing likelihood functions is common. Following this convention, we set $\\mu=\\theta$, and we get\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}\n$$\n\nLet's use this to compute some likelihood functions of some normally distributed sample data." 13194139534866 17592186045417 false] [17592186193902 1064 "The probability density function of a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSome example normal distribution curves are shown below. \n\n![](images/c9/c9359fc84401aabb19e526755b70647cb0496fa8ddba65974466e0ffc3dbbbdc.png)\n\nIf we set $\\sigma=1,$ then $f(x)$ simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a standard normal random variable whose mean is shifted right by $\\mu$ units.\n\nFinally, setting the unknown parameter as $\\theta$ when discussing likelihood functions is common. Following this convention, we set $\\mu=\\theta,$ and we get\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's use this to compute some likelihood functions of some normally distributed sample data." 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441686 1080 "-3.78+3.9\\theta -2.5{\\theta }^{2}" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441686 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 1319413953