}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=18$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=34.2$ and $n=18.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{18}(34.2)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441767 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1069 "q-159950" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=5.6,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=7.8.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{16{\\pi }^{4}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1072 #uuid "0b9cdfe0-b9cf-519c-9814-c1b778856085" 13194139534866 17592186045417 true] [17592186441776 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with